1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,873
Wednesday, the sixth of April, nineteen sixty-six.

2
00:00:03,873 --> 00:00:07,058
A school oval in the south-east of Melbourne.

3
00:00:07,058 --> 00:00:13,571
Two physical education classes are outside, and the bell for morning recess is minutes away.

4
00:00:13,571 --> 00:00:15,199
Then somebody looks up.

5
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:19,848
Some call it the biggest mass sighting in Australian history.

6
00:00:19,848 --> 00:00:21,288
Hundreds of children.

7
00:00:21,288 --> 00:00:21,905
Teachers.

8
00:00:21,905 --> 00:00:28,966
A silver disc, they say, that came down in a paddock, and men in suits who told everyone to keep quiet.

9
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:33,101
But a school day is a wonderful thing to investigate.

10
00:00:33,101 --> 00:00:38,846
It has a timetable, a roll, a staff room, and a local paper that wrote things down.

11
00:00:38,846 --> 00:00:43,000
So tonight we run this case like a school day, bell by bell.

12
00:00:43,466 --> 00:00:44,168
Roll call.

13
00:00:44,168 --> 00:00:45,924
First period on the oval.

14
00:00:45,924 --> 00:00:47,469
Recess in the paddock.

15
00:00:47,469 --> 00:00:48,522
The staff room.

16
00:00:48,522 --> 00:00:49,225
The press.

17
00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:51,612
Detention for the weather balloon.

18
00:00:51,612 --> 00:00:53,649
And a final lesson on memory.

19
00:00:53,649 --> 00:00:55,966
Then the case gets a report card.

20
00:00:56,433 --> 00:01:00,824
Every claim will be marked: evidence, unverified, or failed.

21
00:01:00,824 --> 00:01:03,458
And one question stays on the board.

22
00:01:03,458 --> 00:01:07,995
What did hundreds of schoolchildren actually see over Westall?

23
00:01:07,995 --> 00:01:09,166
Take your seats.

24
00:01:13,633 --> 00:01:14,340
Roll call.

25
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:21,133
Westall High School, in Clayton South, about twenty-one kilometres from the centre of Melbourne.

26
00:01:21,133 --> 00:01:22,053
Flat country.

27
00:01:22,053 --> 00:01:29,200
Low brick classrooms, a big sports oval, and two huge electricity pylons at the edges of the grounds.

28
00:01:29,666 --> 00:01:31,919
It was the Wednesday before Easter.

29
00:01:31,919 --> 00:01:37,068
The first term finished the next afternoon, and then came two weeks of holidays.

30
00:01:37,068 --> 00:01:40,866
Students and teachers alike had their minds somewhere else.

31
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:44,458
And the sky above that school was never empty.

32
00:01:44,458 --> 00:01:51,251
Moorabbin Airport lay about four kilometres away, one of the busiest little airports in the country.

33
00:01:51,251 --> 00:01:55,666
Every student knew what a light aircraft looked and sounded like.

34
00:01:56,133 --> 00:02:00,998
To the south lay The Grange, a patch of scrub, paddocks and pine trees.

35
00:02:00,998 --> 00:02:02,917
Farmers grazed cattle there.

36
00:02:02,917 --> 00:02:06,343
The school ran its cross-country races through it.

37
00:02:06,343 --> 00:02:07,714
Remember this place.

38
00:02:07,714 --> 00:02:09,633
It matters in a few minutes.

39
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:16,523
This was also the age of the space race, the Cold War, and flying saucers in the headlines.

40
00:02:16,523 --> 00:02:22,947
Everyone in nineteen sixty-six knew exactly what a flying saucer was supposed to look like.

41
00:02:22,947 --> 00:02:24,500
Keep that in mind too.

42
00:02:24,966 --> 00:02:26,231
That is the stage.

43
00:02:26,231 --> 00:02:28,410
Now, what happened on the oval?

44
00:02:28,410 --> 00:02:29,394
The bell goes.

45
00:02:29,394 --> 00:02:30,800
First period begins.

46
00:02:31,266 --> 00:02:32,151
First period.

47
00:02:32,151 --> 00:02:44,599
At about a quarter past ten, by one careful account, two groups of Form One and Form Three students were out on the oval for sport, with their teachers, among them Miss Jeanette Muir.

48
00:02:45,066 --> 00:02:46,518
Then came shouting.

49
00:02:46,518 --> 00:02:48,658
A few students saw it first.

50
00:02:48,658 --> 00:02:53,473
Silent, silver or grey, domed on top, like an upside-down bowl.

51
00:02:53,473 --> 00:02:56,071
About the size of one car, or two.

52
00:02:56,071 --> 00:02:57,599
And no sound at all.

53
00:02:58,066 --> 00:03:03,593
Some say it passed low over the oval, as low as the gum trees and the goalposts.

54
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:06,840
Others say it stayed far off, beyond the pines.

55
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:10,433
Already, two different versions of the same morning.

56
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:16,461
A girl ran into a science lesson shouting that there were flying saucers outside.

57
00:03:16,461 --> 00:03:19,689
Her teacher, Andrew Greenwood, was not pleased.

58
00:03:19,689 --> 00:03:24,633
But when the recess bell rang, he took his class out to see for himself.

59
00:03:25,099 --> 00:03:28,667
Greenwood gave a detailed account to the local paper.

60
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:34,995
He saw a silvery streak, like a thin beam of light, about half the length of a light aircraft.

61
00:03:34,995 --> 00:03:38,900
It moved from side to side, and its size seemed to change.

62
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:40,686
And then the planes.

63
00:03:40,686 --> 00:03:46,956
Five light aircraft, he said, chasing it in a game of cat and mouse that lasted twenty minutes.

64
00:03:46,956 --> 00:03:52,566
On twenty-first of April the paper asked on its front page: who were the five pilots?

65
00:03:53,033 --> 00:03:54,577
The Age asked around.

66
00:03:54,577 --> 00:04:00,829
No commercial, private or Air Force pilot had reported anything unusual that morning.

67
00:04:00,829 --> 00:04:06,566
If five aircraft had been chasing something, nobody has ever said it was them.

68
00:04:07,033 --> 00:04:08,933
Later retellings add more.

69
00:04:08,933 --> 00:04:11,125
Some witnesses saw one object.

70
00:04:11,125 --> 00:04:12,075
Some saw two.

71
00:04:12,075 --> 00:04:12,951
Some, three.

72
00:04:12,951 --> 00:04:19,966
It rose, they say, and vanished in a blink, leaving the five planes behind as if standing still.

73
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:28,294
One account survives from that April, an anonymous report in a student magazine from a nearby primary school.

74
00:04:28,294 --> 00:04:32,838
It guessed the craft might be military aircraft from Moorabbin.

75
00:04:32,838 --> 00:04:36,733
It was reprinted that June in a saucer-society review.

76
00:04:37,199 --> 00:04:41,664
And now the craft, they said, dropped down behind the pine trees.

77
00:04:41,664 --> 00:04:43,381
The recess bell had rung.

78
00:04:43,381 --> 00:04:47,433
Dozens of students jumped the fence and ran for The Grange.

79
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:48,392
Recess.

80
00:04:48,392 --> 00:04:52,048
Across a dirt road, over paddocks, toward the pines.

81
00:04:52,048 --> 00:04:54,790
Teachers shouted for them to come back.

82
00:04:54,790 --> 00:04:57,391
Many did not hear, or did not listen.

83
00:04:57,391 --> 00:05:00,766
It was the most exciting morning of their lives.

84
00:05:01,233 --> 00:05:06,839
A few witnesses remember girls who fainted, and an ambulance on the school oval.

85
00:05:06,839 --> 00:05:10,343
Some remember a shape lifting off as they arrived.

86
00:05:10,343 --> 00:05:13,566
Others arrived just after, and saw only grass.

87
00:05:14,033 --> 00:05:16,887
What they found, many say, was a circle.

88
00:05:16,887 --> 00:05:19,883
Flattened grass, swirled in one direction.

89
00:05:19,883 --> 00:05:21,382
Some called it burnt.

90
00:05:21,382 --> 00:05:22,595
Some said boiled.

91
00:05:22,595 --> 00:05:24,949
Some said three circles, not one.

92
00:05:24,949 --> 00:05:26,733
The details never agreed.

93
00:05:27,199 --> 00:05:34,021
One student, interviewed by the local paper at the time, thought it looked like the wind had flattened it.

94
00:05:34,021 --> 00:05:39,233
In nineteen sixty-six, that was one of the very few records of the circle itself.

95
00:05:39,699 --> 00:05:43,676
The woodwork teacher, Gerry Shepherd, had not seen a saucer.

96
00:05:43,676 --> 00:05:45,532
But he knew The Grange well.

97
00:05:45,532 --> 00:05:51,100
In twenty years he never saw a circle like that one, he later said, before or since.

98
00:05:51,566 --> 00:06:01,938
Decades later, a firefighter and a civil defence officer told researchers their crews were sent to The Grange that day, to something reported coming down.

99
00:06:01,938 --> 00:06:04,699
No call log has been shown to the public.

100
00:06:05,166 --> 00:06:07,411
Here is the first strange thing.

101
00:06:07,411 --> 00:06:10,850
Hundreds of children, in an age of cheap cameras.

102
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:17,866
And yet no authenticated photograph or film of the object, or the circle, has ever been made public.

103
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:24,985
Three days later, on the ninth of April, Air Force personnel and saucer enthusiasts visited the field.

104
00:06:24,985 --> 00:06:27,202
They reported nothing of interest.

105
00:06:27,202 --> 00:06:31,833
The landowner then burned the paddock, to keep people off his property.

106
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:35,699
Some versions say the military burned the evidence.

107
00:06:35,699 --> 00:06:38,433
The farmer's own explanation was simpler.

108
00:06:38,433 --> 00:06:43,233
Either way, the one place that could have held a physical clue was gone.

109
00:06:43,699 --> 00:06:50,829
So by the end of recess: lights or discs in the sky, a vague circle, and nothing anyone could pick up.

110
00:06:50,829 --> 00:06:53,065
But the school day was not over.

111
00:06:53,065 --> 00:06:54,533
Next, the staff room.

112
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:55,967
The staff room.

113
00:06:55,967 --> 00:06:59,904
The headmaster, Frank Samblebe, called the students together.

114
00:06:59,904 --> 00:07:08,100
According to many witnesses, he told them that flying saucers did not exist, and that they were not to talk about this morning.

115
00:07:08,566 --> 00:07:11,415
Some recall strangers standing beside him.

116
00:07:11,415 --> 00:07:12,839
Dark suits, say some.

117
00:07:12,839 --> 00:07:14,942
Dark blue uniforms, say others.

118
00:07:14,942 --> 00:07:17,723
All agree, they were from the Government.

119
00:07:17,723 --> 00:07:22,199
Some students say they were questioned in the headmaster's office.

120
00:07:22,666 --> 00:07:25,107
Notice what kind of evidence this is.

121
00:07:25,107 --> 00:07:28,405
Every word of it comes from memory, decades later.

122
00:07:28,405 --> 00:07:32,100
So we go back to what was printed in nineteen sixty-six.

123
00:07:32,566 --> 00:07:35,556
The Dandenong Journal, fourteenth of April.

124
00:07:35,556 --> 00:07:38,962
Front page: Flying Saucer Mystery, School Silent.

125
00:07:38,962 --> 00:07:47,166
The school, it reported, would allow no further interviews, and had asked students and staff not to talk to reporters.

126
00:07:47,633 --> 00:07:52,559
Three weeks later, the same paper printed the headmaster's own explanation.

127
00:07:52,559 --> 00:08:01,033
The flood of callers, he said, from the Air Force down to the Flying Saucer Association, was interrupting the children's studies.

128
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:02,658
Read that twice.

129
00:08:02,658 --> 00:08:06,497
A headmaster protecting his school, say the skeptics.

130
00:08:06,497 --> 00:08:08,743
A silencing, say the believers.

131
00:08:08,743 --> 00:08:12,727
And the very same newspaper line supports both stories.

132
00:08:12,727 --> 00:08:14,899
That is how this case behaves.

133
00:08:15,366 --> 00:08:17,563
Later accounts go much further.

134
00:08:17,563 --> 00:08:26,561
Twenty-four witnesses have told researchers of police or soldiers at the school or the paddock, of cordons, of Geiger counters.

135
00:08:26,561 --> 00:08:30,033
None of this appears in the papers of that April.

136
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,147
And the official files?

137
00:08:32,147 --> 00:08:37,732
No Air Force, police or government file about Westall has been publicly found.

138
00:08:37,732 --> 00:08:41,957
Either nothing was written, or what was written is missing.

139
00:08:41,957 --> 00:08:43,533
Silence can mean both.

140
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:44,672
The press.

141
00:08:44,672 --> 00:08:46,151
Two newspapers matter.

142
00:08:46,151 --> 00:08:55,766
The first is the big Melbourne daily, The Age, and its short item on the seventh of April, with a very plain headline: Object, Perhaps Balloon.

143
00:08:56,233 --> 00:09:07,399
The Weather Bureau, it said, had released a balloon from Laverton at half past eight that morning, and the westerly wind could have carried it into the area where the object was seen.

144
00:09:07,866 --> 00:09:12,950
The second paper is the local Dandenong Journal, the only one to dig in.

145
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:21,000
Brian Dunning, a skeptic, notes that in its first reports it named one teacher and several students, not hundreds.

146
00:09:21,466 --> 00:09:29,995
A saucer society, the Victorian Flying Saucer Research Society, inspected the scene and interviewed witnesses that April.

147
00:09:29,995 --> 00:09:32,744
But no full account was published then.

148
00:09:32,744 --> 00:09:35,000
The thick files came much later.

149
00:09:35,466 --> 00:09:40,170
Researcher Shane Ryan began collecting testimony in the two thousands.

150
00:09:40,170 --> 00:09:52,533
By the sixtieth anniversary, his count stood at one hundred and forty-two who saw the objects, one hundred and ninety-seven who saw marks on the ground, and seventy-seven who saw both.

151
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,093
His work fed a two thousand and ten documentary, Westall sixty-six.

152
00:09:58,093 --> 00:10:08,433
Remarkably, it was later used in Australian classrooms as a lesson in critical analysis: how do we judge a popular story about the past?

153
00:10:08,899 --> 00:10:09,601
Detention.

154
00:10:09,601 --> 00:10:12,898
Time to put the ordinary explanations on trial.

155
00:10:12,898 --> 00:10:20,967
There are two main ones, and they are each stronger than most believers admit, and weaker than most skeptics claim.

156
00:10:20,967 --> 00:10:22,299
First, the balloon.

157
00:10:22,766 --> 00:10:38,200
Between nineteen sixty and sixty-nine, Australia and the United States flew HIBAL balloons, large silver research balloons carrying instruments to sample the air for radiation after the British nuclear tests at Maralinga.

158
00:10:38,666 --> 00:10:47,974
Researcher Keith Basterfield found papers pointing to a HIBAL flight planned from Mildura on the fifth of April, the day before Westall.

159
00:10:47,974 --> 00:10:52,833
But the report on that launch, and where it landed, has not been found.

160
00:10:53,299 --> 00:10:55,364
The theory fits some details.

161
00:10:55,364 --> 00:11:03,122
A silver shape, a parachute, a metal payload, and, in this programme, a light aircraft following to track it.

162
00:11:03,122 --> 00:11:06,966
The trouble is, no document ties any HIBAL to Westall.

163
00:11:07,433 --> 00:11:09,433
Critics add two more problems.

164
00:11:09,433 --> 00:11:14,366
A heavy payload at low level would hardly dart about faster than aircraft.

165
00:11:14,366 --> 00:11:22,366
And a recent review of the weather data, reported for the sixtieth anniversary, says the weather data does not stack up.

166
00:11:22,833 --> 00:11:30,511
The second explanation came from a letter in the Dandenong Journal, signed only by a former Air Force navigator.

167
00:11:30,511 --> 00:11:38,600
Greenwood's chase, he wrote, matched a trainer towing a nylon target drogue while other pilots practised following it.

168
00:11:39,066 --> 00:11:48,063
A target drogue would explain a silvery streak, a cat-and-mouse chase, and why pilots saw nothing odd in a routine exercise.

169
00:11:48,063 --> 00:11:52,489
But no record shows such an exercise took place that morning.

170
00:11:52,489 --> 00:11:54,666
It remains a possibility only.

171
00:11:55,133 --> 00:11:56,749
And the alien version?

172
00:11:56,749 --> 00:12:01,155
It needs a craft that landed, left a circle, and left again.

173
00:12:01,155 --> 00:12:04,460
No craft, no sample, no radar, no photograph.

174
00:12:04,460 --> 00:12:06,369
Not one piece of hardware.

175
00:12:06,369 --> 00:12:08,866
Everything it relies on is memory.

176
00:12:09,333 --> 00:12:11,870
So maybe the answer is not one thing.

177
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:18,041
Dunning suggests a balloon for the first half of the sighting and aircraft for the second.

178
00:12:18,041 --> 00:12:18,659
Possible.

179
00:12:18,659 --> 00:12:21,333
But it is a guess built on two guesses.

180
00:12:21,799 --> 00:12:23,282
Final lesson: memory.

181
00:12:23,282 --> 00:12:28,295
In nineteen sixty-six these witnesses were twelve to sixteen years old.

182
00:12:28,295 --> 00:12:32,814
The first systematic interviews came in the early two thousands.

183
00:12:32,814 --> 00:12:37,333
Four decades of retelling lay between the oval and the notebook.

184
00:12:37,799 --> 00:12:46,562
Skeptic Richard Saunders, reviewing the case in twenty twenty-six, calls it memory fragility, false memory and memory contagion.

185
00:12:46,562 --> 00:12:49,849
Yet he also says he believes something happened.

186
00:12:49,849 --> 00:12:53,066
He just thinks the details grew in the telling.

187
00:12:53,533 --> 00:12:56,202
The reports from the time do differ.

188
00:12:56,202 --> 00:12:57,537
A weather balloon.

189
00:12:57,537 --> 00:12:59,094
A humped silver disc.

190
00:12:59,094 --> 00:13:00,577
One object, or five.

191
00:13:00,577 --> 00:13:02,060
One circle, or many.

192
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:07,399
Every group of honest witnesses ends up with a slightly different story.

193
00:13:07,866 --> 00:13:10,585
And yet, listen to them at sixty years on.

194
00:13:10,585 --> 00:13:12,980
These are not people selling a story.

195
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:20,099
Many have carried it quietly all their lives, and say only that they would like an answer from the government.

196
00:13:20,566 --> 00:13:22,540
What survives every filter?

197
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:28,242
A silent, silvery object, seen by many people from a school in broad daylight.

198
00:13:28,242 --> 00:13:30,216
Light aircraft in the area.

199
00:13:30,216 --> 00:13:33,433
The rest is thin, contradictory, or missing.

200
00:13:33,899 --> 00:13:42,569
Calls for a fresh inquiry are growing, with researchers pointing to Department of Supply files they say are still unopened.

201
00:13:42,569 --> 00:13:46,234
If those papers exist, they could end this argument.

202
00:13:46,234 --> 00:13:48,066
Until then, it stays open.

203
00:13:48,533 --> 00:13:49,380
Report card.

204
00:13:49,380 --> 00:13:51,500
Time to mark the Westall case.

205
00:13:51,966 --> 00:13:56,000
Many people saw something odd that morning: evidence.

206
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,045
Reported in print within days: evidence.

207
00:13:59,045 --> 00:14:02,394
Accounts that agree on details: only partly.

208
00:14:02,394 --> 00:14:06,733
A burned landing circle: unverified, no photo, no sample.

209
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:12,742
Military cordon and men in suits: nothing in the 1966 record.

210
00:14:12,742 --> 00:14:16,559
Balloon or drogue: plausible but unproven.

211
00:14:16,559 --> 00:14:19,466
Alien craft: no evidence at all.

212
00:14:19,933 --> 00:14:20,782
The verdict.

213
00:14:20,782 --> 00:14:21,561
Incomplete.

214
00:14:21,561 --> 00:14:28,287
Something unusual really was seen over Westall, by real people, and the story was told at once.

215
00:14:28,287 --> 00:14:32,181
But nothing in the record shows a craft from elsewhere.

216
00:14:32,181 --> 00:14:35,154
Most likely a mix of balloon and aircraft.

217
00:14:35,154 --> 00:14:36,499
See me after class.

218
00:14:36,966 --> 00:14:43,842
Today, The Grange has a children's playground shaped like a silver flying saucer, with red slides.

219
00:14:43,842 --> 00:14:46,648
A small joke from the people of Westall.

220
00:14:46,648 --> 00:14:50,366
And a reminder that this story is, above all, theirs.

221
00:14:50,833 --> 00:14:58,345
Next case: a policeman on a lonely New Mexico road, an egg-shaped craft in a gully, and a burned bush.

222
00:14:58,345 --> 00:15:00,039
Was it real, or a hoax?

223
00:15:00,039 --> 00:15:01,733
We open that file next.

224
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:04,288
What do you think the children saw?

225
00:15:04,288 --> 00:15:09,299
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
